From 0c3577584a35c682bbad4c64f53a211d1581f8cb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daaboulex <39669593+Daaboulex@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 12:49:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(cpufreq): add a generic cpufreq boost driver

Machines whose cpufreq scaling driver has no energy performance
preference interface only get the placeholder driver today, so the
CPU ignores the selected profile and there is no performance profile.
On Apple Silicon (apple-cpufreq) the global boost switch gates the
turbo frequencies, see "Frequency Boost Support" in
https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/pm/cpufreq.html

Add a CPU driver that turns boost off in power-saver and on in
balanced and performance; a failed write restores the previous value.
It only claims a machine when every cpufreq policy whose scaling
driver can be read runs one on an explicit list, currently only
apple-cpufreq, so every other machine, including every x86 one, keeps
the driver it uses today. A policy that cannot be read is skipped,
since only one cpufreq driver can be registered at a time. The probe
opens the boost switch for writing without writing to it. Like the
boost handling in amd_pstate, the switch does not depend on the power
source, so there is no power_changed handler.

Integration tests cover the boost switching, the scaling drivers it
leaves alone, unreadable policies, a read-only boost switch, and
yielding to amd_pstate.

This follows up !117 (apple-cpufreq), closed for inactivity, whose
review asked for a generic driver with integration tests.

Test: meson test with --werror and pylint, 138/139 (the pylint check
of powerprofilesctl fails the same on unpatched main); ASan and UBSan
137/137; the new tests fail with the fixes reverted; on an M1 Max
power-saver caps the P-cores at 3036 MHz, balanced and performance
return them to 3228 MHz
